  "worked_solution": "Let $p$ be the perimeter of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $p$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\np=2\\sqrt{2}h.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(2\\sqrt{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each diagonal of the square is increasing at a rate of $9\\sqrt{2}\\text{\u00a0cm}\\text{/s},$ so we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=9\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information into the above expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =(9\\sqrt{2})\\cdot (2\\sqrt{2})=36\\text{\u00a0cm/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter of the square increases at $36\\text{\u00a0cm}/\\text{s}.$",
